import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'FAQ | hostileworkcalc.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about hostile work environment claims: severity standard, employer liability, EEOC filing, and damages caps.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>What makes a work environment legally &ldquo;hostile&rdquo;?</h2>
  <p>The conduct must be (1) based on a protected characteristic, (2) objectively severe or pervasive enough to alter working conditions, and (3) subjectively offensive to the plaintiff. Rude behavior, personal conflicts, or general unfairness are not enough — the conduct must be linked to race, sex, religion, national origin, age, or disability.</p>
  <h2>Is one incident enough?</h2>
  <p>One incident can be enough if it is sufficiently severe — for example, a single sexual assault, a physical attack, or an extremely serious racial slur. Less severe incidents generally require a pattern over time to meet the pervasiveness standard.</p>
  <h2>Can I sue my coworker directly?</h2>
  <p>Under Title VII, individual employees (including harassers) are generally not personally liable — liability runs to the employer. Some states allow individual liability for supervisors. You can name the harasser in some state court claims.</p>
  <h2>Does the company have to know about the harassment?</h2>
  <p>For supervisor harassment: if the supervisor&rsquo;s harassment results in a tangible employment action (firing, demotion), the employer is automatically liable. If no tangible action, the employer can assert the Faragher/Ellerth affirmative defense (they had a reasonable anti-harassment policy and the plaintiff unreasonably failed to use it). For co-worker harassment: the employer is liable only if it knew or should have known and failed to take prompt corrective action.</p>
  <h2>Must I use the internal complaint process first?</h2>
  <p>Using the internal complaint process is strongly advisable. It creates a record, triggers the employer&rsquo;s obligation to investigate, and protects you against the Faragher/Ellerth defense. It also protects against retaliation (reporting is protected activity). For EEOC purposes, you must exhaust administrative remedies before filing a federal lawsuit.</p>
</BaseLayout>
